+**Memory in a designed workflow.** Agents do not use memory well on their own: their tool loads a fixed set of
+files, and anything past that is read only if the model decides to, which in measured runs it often does not
+(`runs/20261013-memory-in-workflow-design/report.md`, E1, E2). So the design decides, for every node, what memory
+reaches it and what it writes; nothing is left to a reminder the agent may skip. Project memory follows
+`docs/memory-system/SOURCE_OF_TRUTH.md` and its caches follow `docs/memory-system/CACHES.md`; the method does not
+repeat their rules. It applies them per node, in the fields every node already has (3.6; the skill's "Every node
+carries five things"):
 
-| File (`docs/blueprint/`) | Answers | Entry (kept to about 5 lines) | Written by, when |
-|---|---|---|---|
-| `decisions.md` | "Why did we choose X? What did we reject?" | date · decision · why · alternatives rejected · the R/N ids it serves · source (finding ids, or *model knowledge*, 3.8 item 12) | the COO, when a decision spot is answered (3.3) or a design choice is made; EJ's answers are the decisions |
-| `changelog.md` | "What did run N change, and did it pass?" | date · run id · what changed · criteria covered (R1.1…) · commits · baseline result · optional `release note:` line for users | the COO, once at the end of each run that builds, from the state file |
-| `lessons.md` | "What goes wrong around module Y?" | date · where (path or module) · the trap · how it was found · recheck by (a date or a trigger) | any node proposes, the COO appends; an entry past its recheck is marked stale, never silently trusted |
-
-Working rules: append only, never rewritten (a reversal is a new entry that points at the old one); every entry
-carries a date and, where it applies, a requirement id or a path, so an agent finds it by `grep`, not by reading the
-file; readiness (3.1, information) and the design read only the entries for the sections, ids and paths the task
-touches; the blueprint's `README.md` map lists the files so an agent knows they exist. A release note, when one is
-needed, is generated from the `release note:` lines of the changelog, not written separately. The qwen findings of
-2026-10-02 (silent model substitution, exit 0 with no file) are examples of lessons.
+| Field | Memory in it |
+|---|---|
+| **Context** — memory in | The entries the node needs, named by id or by the paths they cover, and how they reach it: in the brief, by an import, or by a start hook, never as a sentence asking it to read them (SoT Rd2.1). What it must not receive: a verifier gets no worker's reasoning, and no entry or journal that carries it. |
+| **Contract** — before acting | A node that acts on a decision or fact checks it against the code first; a mismatch is reported in the state file and to EJ, not settled (SoT Rd5). |
+| **State** — memory out | What the node writes, and when: a decision or fact before the code it governs (W5.1); `done:` on a decision when its work is complete (W5.3); raw notes to the task's `journal/`, turned into lessons, observations and hypotheses at the end of the task (K5.2); `INDEX.md` regenerated in the same commit as any entry (K6.2). The node's return lists the entries it wrote, and its check fails if a required one is missing. |
+| **Tools** — the caches | A cache is never loaded at start (CACHES Rd1). A node uses full-text search or the vector index only for a free-text "what do we know about X" question, and only when that cache exists (its trigger fired, B1); otherwise `INDEX.md`, grep, then read the entry. Exact strings, "every entry covering X", "have we never…", header fields and "why" questions go to the files, never to a cache (CACHES § When to use which store). A cache answer names its entry, and the node reads that entry before acting on it (Rd2). |
+
+An engine's own built-in memory (Claude Code auto memory, a subagent's `memory` field, Codex memories) is not part of
+the design: a rule a node must follow lives in the repo files that reach it, never only in one engine's memory (report
+M14, T6).
 
